From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thomas Lamprecht <t.lamprecht@proxmox.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 22:29:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] PVE backup: vma reader: reject zero-length name blobs

The check for the terminating NUL byte of a name indexes the blob data
with its length minus one. As the length is unsigned, a zero-length
blob wraps that index to almost 4 GiB past the blob data. A crafted
archive that references such a blob as a device or config name thus
crashes vma, or makes it read unrelated memory, already while parsing
the header, for example with 'vma list'.

Treat a zero-length blob like a name without a terminating NUL byte,
which is already considered invalid. Empty names, stored as a single
NUL byte, are not affected.

Signed-off-by: Thomas Lamprecht <t.lamprecht@proxmox.com>
---
 vma-reader.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/vma-reader.c b/vma-reader.c
index 1888b21851..ebd17c907c 100644
--- a/vma-reader.c
+++ b/vma-reader.c
@@ -123,7 +123,7 @@ static const VmaBlob *get_header_blob(VmaReader *vmar, uint32_t pos)
 static const char *get_header_str(VmaReader *vmar, uint32_t pos)
 {
     const VmaBlob *blob = get_header_blob(vmar, pos);
-    if (!blob) {
+    if (!blob || !blob->len) {
         return NULL;
     }
     const char *res = (char *)blob->data;
